{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And it shall be</b> — [the vav is conjunction] and 'the rain shall not be upon them' — like weak kaf-peh-heh in the language of Ishmael, and similar to it: 'on the third day,' 'and Abraham lifted up his eyes,' 'and he left his servants,' 'and this stone.' And no human ability can render this in any other language, for this construction is found only in the language of Ishmael and in the sacred tongue." "<b>And the meaning of 'the rain' on Sukkot</b> — as the Sages translated it, to mention the mighty acts of rain and to seek mercy for them."], :book :zechariah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra illustrates the vav-conversive or vav of continuation with examples from Genesis; the argument concerns a Hebrew syntactic feature he claims is paralleled only in Arabic."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Zecharia -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/zecharya"] ["1" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=25&perek=5&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:47:08.018205289Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}